<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dundrennan Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1142</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dundrennan Abbey is a ruined Cistercian abbey near Kirkcudbright in Galloway, south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, founded in 1142 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergus-of-galloway">Fergus of Galloway</a>, perhaps with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">King David</a> I, and settled by monks from Rievaulx. Mary, Queen of Scots spent her last night in Scotland there in May 1568 before crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solway-firth">Solway Firth</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dundrennan-abbey.jpg" alt="Dundrennan Abbey" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Craig Murphy from Scotland · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dundrennan_Abbey_2012_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
